+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-parses-and-validates-the-pointer-before-walking-the-document
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer parses and validates the pointer before walking the document"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` must follow RFC 6901 exactly, and the services that import this
+library depend on it throwing for every invalid pointer and every pointer that does not resolve.
+
+## Decision
+
+The pointer is fully parsed (syntax, escapes, and the leading "/") into reference tokens before any
+part of the document is read. Within the walk, an array token must be an array index (`0` or a
+non-zero digit followed by digits, with no sign and no leading zero), and `-` always throws because it
+never resolves. An object token is looked up only as an own property (`Object.hasOwn`), so inherited
+names such as `constructor` or `__proto__` do not resolve. Any other traversal into a primitive or
+`null` throws.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- An invalid pointer is rejected even when the document would make a partial walk succeed.
+- Callers get one error type for both "invalid pointer" and "does not resolve", with a message that
+  says which one.
+- Prototype names are never returned from a document, which keeps lookups limited to the data.
